WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.020
How much air can your lungs hold?

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:06.551
Today we'll find out, using just a balloon and a ruler.

00:00:07.101 --> 00:00:08.967
We'll measure three lung volumes.

00:00:09.017 --> 00:00:10.169
Tidal volume

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:12.181
Expiratory reserve volume

00:00:12.231 --> 00:00:13.772
And vital capacity

00:00:14.322 --> 00:00:17.303
Each one tells us something different about how we breathe.

00:00:17.953 --> 00:00:20.141
One safety note before we start.

00:00:20.491 --> 00:00:25.302
Anyone with asthma or another breathing difficulty should not collect their own data.

00:00:25.552 --> 00:00:28.865
If that's you, please talk with your teacher before you begin.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.446
First, stretch the balloon five times.

00:00:33.496 --> 00:00:35.544
Pull it wide, then let it relax.

00:00:35.694 --> 00:00:36.535
One.

00:00:36.985 --> 00:00:37.801
Two.

00:00:38.251 --> 00:00:39.168
Three.

00:00:39.618 --> 00:00:40.549
Four.

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:41.935
Five.

00:00:42.385 --> 00:00:45.263
That loosens the rubber, so it's easier to blow up.

00:00:45.713 --> 00:00:49.805
Now lay the balloon flat on the table, and stand the ruler up beside it.

00:00:50.455 --> 00:00:53.111
Set the zero end of the ruler on the table.

00:00:53.761 --> 00:00:56.574
Keep the centimeter side of the ruler facing you.

00:00:57.224 --> 00:01:02.350
We measure the diameter: the distance straight across the balloon at its widest part.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.250
Read the ruler at the top of the balloon. This one is about 8 centimeters.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.567
Hold still for a moment, and read it carefully.

00:01:14.292 --> 00:01:15.961
First, tidal volume.

00:01:16.011 --> 00:01:19.460
That's the air you move in and out with a normal, quiet breath.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.195
So we'll breathe normally, and capture just one breath.

00:01:23.645 --> 00:01:25.280
Take a normal breath in.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:30.969
Now breathe out normally into the balloon. Don't force it.

00:01:33.419 --> 00:01:35.533
Pinch the neck so no air escapes.

00:01:36.583 --> 00:01:37.647
Now measure.

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:41.487
The diameter is about 10 centimeters.

00:01:42.337 --> 00:01:44.682
Say the number out loud as you read it.

00:01:45.532 --> 00:01:47.730
Let's do it a second time, quickly.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:49.792
Normal breath in.

00:01:50.342 --> 00:01:51.744
Normal breath out.

00:01:52.494 --> 00:01:53.390
Pinch.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:54.420
Measure.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.041
About 9.5 centimeters.

00:01:57.891 --> 00:02:01.450
Write down each number. We'll average the trials in a moment.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.705
Tidal volume is the smallest of the three amounts we'll measure.

00:02:08.083 --> 00:02:10.653
Next, expiratory reserve volume.

00:02:10.703 --> 00:02:14.695
That's the extra air you can still push out, after a normal breath out.

00:02:15.545 --> 00:02:17.994
This one is tricky, so watch closely.

00:02:18.444 --> 00:02:21.383
Take a normal breath in, and breathe out normally.

00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:28.490
Now, without breathing in, blow all the extra air into the balloon, and pinch it.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.823
Do NOT take a breath in between!

00:02:34.873 --> 00:02:38.744
Here's what goes wrong. If you breathe in first, you add new air.

00:02:40.394 --> 00:02:45.140
The balloon comes out too big, and that number isn't your expiratory reserve volume.

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.924
So breathe out normally, then blow out the extra air. Measure.

00:02:51.574 --> 00:02:53.286
About 12 centimeters.

00:02:55.833 --> 00:02:57.588
Last, vital capacity.

00:02:57.638 --> 00:03:00.287
That's the most air you can move in one breath.

00:03:01.137 --> 00:03:03.997
It's the biggest breath of all, so take your time.

00:03:04.447 --> 00:03:06.413
Take the deepest breath you can.

00:03:09.063 --> 00:03:11.417
Now blow it all out into the balloon.

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:17.849
Keep going until you can't push out any more air.

00:03:18.899 --> 00:03:20.306
Pinch it, and measure.

00:03:21.356 --> 00:03:23.236
About 18 centimeters.

00:03:23.886 --> 00:03:26.688
Notice how much bigger the balloon is this time.

00:03:27.338 --> 00:03:30.255
Vital capacity is the largest of the three.

00:03:32.375 --> 00:03:34.280
Now let's use the numbers.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:39.296
Why average? One trial can be a little off, and an average evens that out.

00:03:39.946 --> 00:03:46.335
Say three trials gave diameters of 10, 9.5, and 10.5 centimeters.

00:03:47.385 --> 00:03:50.152
Add the three trials. That makes 30.

00:03:51.202 --> 00:03:55.581
Then divide by 3. The average diameter is 10 centimeters.

00:03:57.031 --> 00:04:01.011
Next, use Figure 1 to turn that diameter into a lung volume.

00:04:01.461 --> 00:04:05.755
Figure 1 matches a balloon's diameter to the volume of air inside.

00:04:06.405 --> 00:04:09.161
Find 10 centimeters on the bottom axis.

00:04:10.211 --> 00:04:12.784
Trace straight up, until you reach the curve.

00:04:14.234 --> 00:04:17.007
Then trace straight across, to the volume axis.

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:22.269
Read the lung volume: about 520 milliliters.

00:04:23.519 --> 00:04:26.391
Do the same thing for each diameter you measure.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.817
One more thing to think about. Why can the balloon number come out low?

00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:36.814
First, a little air can leak out before you pinch the neck.

00:04:39.464 --> 00:04:43.878
Second, the balloon pushes back on the air inside, and it isn't a perfect ball.

00:04:46.928 --> 00:04:51.784
Keep both ideas in mind for the question about why the balloon value is lowest.
